  - TypeFullName: Basis.Network.Core.BasisSimdCapabilities
    MemberName: BasisSimdCapabilities
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 1
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "What vector width and instruction sets this process actually got, for the boot log.\n\n \n\nEvery vectorised path in the server is written against Vector{T} and\n selected by the JIT at runtime, which means the same binary runs 16, 32 or 64 bytes at a time\n depending on the host and nothing in the build says which. That is the right trade \u2014 one\n implementation, ARM included \u2014 but it makes the width invisible exactly when someone is trying\n to explain a throughput difference between two machines. Printing it once at boot is the whole\n point of this type.\n\n\n\nTwo traps worth knowing before changing how the server is built:\n\n\n- ReadyToRun / AOT silently costs the vector width. R2R precompiles\n against a conservative ISA baseline (SSE2-era on x64), so `Vector<byte>.Count` is\n baked at 16 and the AVX2 path is never generated. Tiered compilation does re-JIT hot methods at\n the real width, but a startup-time optimisation that quietly halves the width of every vector\n loop until then is not free. If R2R is ever adopted here, publish with an explicit\n `<PublishReadyToRunUseCrossgen2>` instruction-set baseline and re-measure \u2014 do not\n assume it is a pure win.\n- 512-bit Vector{T} is opt-in, and only from the environment.\n The runtime caps Vector{T} at 256 bits on every x64 host and widens it only when\n `DOTNET_MaxVectorTBitWidth=512` is set before the process starts; it reads that knob from\n the environment alone, so nothing in runtimeconfig or in Main can flip it. That is a separate\n decision from whether 512-bit is accelerated at all: `Vector512.IsHardwareAccelerated` is\n already true on AVX-512 hosts except the ones the runtime knows down-clock (Skylake-SP, Cascade\n Lake, Cooper Lake, Cannon Lake), where `DOTNET_PreferredVectorBitWidth=512` is the override\n and the automatic 256 preference also clamps Vector{T} back down. Measured on a\n 7950X3D (Zen 4 double-pumps 512-bit ops), 64-byte Vector{T} is a wash on both vector\n loops here and the codec and sweep tests pass at that width, so the knob is left to the launch\n environment for an A/B on a host with a native 512-bit datapath rather than set by default."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
