  - TypeFullName: Configuration
    MemberName: BSRMaxDegreeOfParallelism
    Kind: Field
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "Worker cap for the BSR tick's parallel phases (send loop, message processing, distance\n sweep). 0 = auto (a quarter of the cores, floored at 4 and capped at 8).\n\n More workers is not free: the tick runs ~275x/s, so each phase pays dispatch and wake cost\n per tick per worker, and every extra thread adds GC poll-point traffic. Measured at 500\n players on a 32-thread box: 32 workers = 11.0 cores, 16 = 8.6, 8 = 6.6, 4 = 6.4, at equal\n or better throughput. Raise it if you run far more players per instance than that and the\n profile shows the send loop itself saturating."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
  - TypeFullName: Configuration
    MemberName: BSRSendPhaseBudgetPercent
    Kind: Field
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "Share of the BSR tick period the send pass is sized against, as a percentage. 0 = the\n fitted default of 60. Clamped to 20..85.\n\n The send pool's width comes from a throughput rate this host measures for itself, so what\n is left to choose is not how fast a worker is but how many of the period's milliseconds the\n pass may spend - that many pairs per millisecond, for that many milliseconds, is a worker\n count. The remainder is not spare: the queue drain, message processing, the distance slice\n and the transport kick run in the same tick, and what those cost is a property of the box,\n which is why this is a setting rather than the constant it used to be. Too high and the\n send pass fits its budget while the tick overruns anyway, which the load controller answers\n by shedding players; too low and the pool is sized wider than the machine while the tick\n sits half idle.\n\n Nothing in the process can fit this, because the send pass has no view of what the phases\n beside it cost. BasisServerBenchmark measures that split under load and writes the value."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
  - TypeFullName: Configuration
    MemberName: BSRMaxSliceCount
    Kind: Field
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "Furthest the reduction system may slice its roster under load. 0 = scale with population.\n\n Slicing is the last-resort lever: at slice N each tick serves only 1/N of the receivers, so\n everyone's update rate drops uniformly. The cap decides how far the server is allowed to\n degrade before it stops degrading and simply overruns its tick instead.\n\n It was a fixed 32, chosen when 2000 was a large instance. At 8000 players a cap of 32 still\n leaves 250 receivers per tick, so a struggling server reaches the ceiling with nowhere left\n to go and starts missing the period \u2014 which is the failure slicing exists to prevent.\n Automatic holds the per-tick fan-out roughly flat as population grows.\n\n Set a positive value only to pin it; higher caps trade update rate for keeping the tick."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
