  - TypeFullName: Basis.Shims.BasisJiggleEventShim
    MemberName: BasisJiggleEventShim
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: CilboxShim
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "Lets a sandboxed script react when a player handles this object's jiggle physics \u2014 the avatar,\n prop or world object can answer back when someone grabs its hair or rests a hand on it\n (issue #685).\n\n Declare either callback and opt in once from `Start`:\n ```\n\n void Start() { GetComponent<BasisJiggleEventShim>(); }\n void OnJiggleGrab( int playerId, bool began, int hand, int rigIndex ) { }\n void OnJiggleTouch( int playerId, bool began, int hand, int rigIndex ) { }\n \n```\n A no-argument version of either is accepted too, for scripts that only care that it happened.\n Cilbox creates the component on demand, so fetching it is the whole opt-in.\n\n Nothing is detected until a script asks. Registering is what turns detection on for\n this object, and the framework does no per-frame work at all while no shim is registered, so\n content that does not use this costs nothing in a crowded room.\n\n Touch means a hand is close enough to grab, measured with the same palm-to-fingertip grip\n volume grabbing uses. Both callbacks are edge triggered: one call when it starts, one when it\n ends, with a short dwell so a resting hand cannot spam the interpreter. Only plain numbers\n cross the boundary \u2014 no transform, player or simulation handle is ever handed over.\n\n This type is method-restricted in `CilboxSceneBasis.extraMethodWhitelist`,\n `CilboxAvatarBasis.extraMethodWhitelist` and `CilboxPropBasis.extraMethodWhitelist`."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
